ut they do each have some some  great sections on the Epicureans so I'll  begin with one of my favorites the  morality of happiness by Julia honest  who's a real giant in the field she's  got I mean there's a lot of really great  stuff in here but she's got some  sections specifically on the Epicureans  and not just in one part of the book  well you know over and over again she's  she's looking at the epicurean stuff and  she has got a lot of great articles on  epicurean tradition as well another  person who gets brought up quite a lot  who's also well worth reading  certainly this work Martha Nussbaum the  therapy of desire theory and practice  and Hellenistic ethics again you're  gonna find you know quite a few things  on Epicureanism there's a chapter  epicurean surgery and then there's  another one mortal immortals which is  about Lucretius the one on Seneca you  know to some degree is addressing the  Epicureans and then finally a book that  came out fairly recently 2006  Christopher gills the structure itself  and Hellenistic and Roman the thought  I'm a big fan of Christopher Gill really  great students of the the Stoics but he  also does good work the Epicureans as  well and there's a lot of cool stuff in  here about the epicurean notion of the  self reconstructing that so you might  find those sorts of things useful I  would also say you know when we're  talking about the Epicureans it might be  interesting to you to not focus just on  say recent or even you know 20th century  secondary literature and  reinterpretations of the Epicureans you  might also take a look at  the way the Epicureans were  reinterpreted not just in people like  ascend II but also more recent authors  John Stuart Mill is viewing the  Epicureans as precursors to the  utilitarian tradition that he belongs to  Karl Marx you know wrote his early work  his dissertation was on when Epicurus  right and and so there in Democritus  right and you know there's there's quite  a few other thinkers who are discussing  Epicurus and the epicurean sometimes  they're they're a little dismissive you  know for example Hegel's examination of  the Epicureans or Nietzsche's you know  almost dismissal of the Epicureans but  occasionally you'll find some some  thinkers like I mentioned David Hume has  a very interesting essay the on the  epicurean that you could check out as  well so you you might find that stuff  giving you interesting points of view  that you can consider and it'll also  help to sort of connect the  constellation in the history of ideas so  you'll see the effect that the epicurean  literature has had throughout its you  know the long history of philosophy and  its connection to other disciplines  over the years I've seen a lot of people  approaching philosophical schools and  thinkers for the first time and there  there are often some problems that will  arise that aren't really that that worth  worrying about let alone getting bogged  down in and with the epicurean school I  think that we can say that there's  there's some issues like that as well I  wouldn't allow myself to become overly  concerned with the polemics about  Epicureans and religion as I pointed out  the Epicureans were in fact theists they  weren't atheists although a lot of  people portray them as such but they  were theists of a really strange sort  who thought that God or the gods don't  really care about the world that the  divine is disconnected and I would you  know I mentioned the swerve and and the  sort of you know it gets criticized for  bringing in a Voltaire Ian's perspective  Voltaire was not a good historian a full  of philosophy or of ideas in general he  was one of the people who bought into  this you know it was all dark ages in  the Middle Ages  I would I would leave all that death  stuff aside I wouldn't worry about that  unless you're already you know  preoccupied with that it's just kind of  a distraction and it doesn't contribute  much to understanding Epicureanism or to  perhaps even trying it out in your own  life another thing that you don't need  to do is stake out a position on other  criticisms of Epicureanism so I  mentioned that like you know we know a  bit about Epicureanism from people like  Cicero or Plutarch or Lucian or even  locked-on so yes I mean if you're  reading locked-on see us more power to  you right kind of a rare taste these  days and you know you don't have to  worry about whether they're totally fair  have it right or anything like that in  order for you to you know acquire a good  conception of what the Epicureans  actually taught I mean it could be that  Cicero is being unfair I think he's on  to the Epicureans at points but we can  still learn from it we don't have to say  Oh a hostile critic therefore we dismiss  everything that would be really foolish  on our part right which from an  epicurean point of view it would be a  bad thing because the Epicureans stress  the virtue of prudence or practical  wisdom the opposite of which is  foolishness so I don't think you have to  worry about about that or and you don't  have to worry too much about whether  Marx or mill or Hume are getting the  Epicureans completely right just try to  learn from their their interpretations  and then as your is you acquire a better  and better understanding of it that's  when you can form those critical  judgments one other thing that I'll  bring up obviously the epicurean physics  is pretty much just as outmoded as the  Aristotelian one is you know you you  might say well wait a second didn't the  Epicureans you know come up with the  atomic doctrine well I mean Democritus  did and the Epicureans took it from them  but what they mean by atoms isn't  remotely close to what we mean by atoms  and the the epicurean physics you know  their conception of the physical  universe it's it's it's you know it's  nice to imagine but it's you know it's  not really close to natural science and  the president as a matter of fact the  Stoics might be closer than the  Epicureans with their concept of Talmud  and you know this notion of matter and  energy shifting back and forth that we  have but you don't need to worry about  that that is not something that has to  distract you from being able to read and  appreciate and understand these texts so  I would put those sort of issues aside I  table them until later unless you really  like to worry about that sort of stuff  so I do have a few final thoughts about  the epicurean school and Epicurus and  you know your own task of self-directed  study first off I want to say if you are  going to study Epicurus that's really  great and continue it on and it'll help  fill in the picture for you in so many  important ways you don't have to  actually agree with him any more than  you have to agree with Plato or the  Stoics or Cicero or any other thinker in  order to have a good solid appreciation  of what it is that they're saying and  understand how they fit in so that  that's an important thing I think you  know I read a lot of people and I teach  a lot of people who myself I don't agree  with but I find very interesting and the  Epicureans really are an interesting  Bunch  I think it's worth spending some time  thinking about what it is that they've  said and looking at their assertions and  arguments and distinctions it will make  you a better thinker whether you agree  with them or not so I you know want to  say one other thing too you know maybe a  word of encouragement if you're watching  this it's probably because you're  interested in carrying out some  self-directed you could say autonomous  study and you were just looking for a  bit of guidance in how to do that I  think that's really great and I hope  that you enjoy reading Epicurus and his  followers and successors and  interpreters and that it proves to be a  fruitful and useful you know way of  devoting yourself to philosophical study  you
 

